C = [
("h1", "10. The Marriage Figure in Paul’s Letters"),
("p", "Claim: Paul uses the figure of marriage in three places, and in each it serves an instruction. In Romans 7 it shows how death releases a person from the law. In 2 Corinthians 11 it shows a pastor’s jealousy for the loyalty of the people he won. In Ephesians 5 it shows husbands and wives how to live and shows what Christ’s love for the church is like. In none of them does Paul call the church the bride or the Lamb’s wife, and in none does he cite the prophets’ wife as the pattern of the church. The figure is real, and the instruction is real. The identity is not given."),
("sub", "10.1 Romans 7:1–4: an illustration about the law"),
("p", "Paul introduces the illustration by naming its hearers: “I speak to them that know the law” (Romans 7:1). The law has dominion over a man as long as he lives, and a wife is bound to her husband as long as he lives. If he dies, she is free, and she may be married to another man without being an adulteress (Romans 7:2–3). The application follows: “Wherefore, my brethren, ye also are become dead to the law by the body of Christ; that ye should be married to another, even to him who is raised from the dead, that we should bring forth fruit unto God” (Romans 7:4)."),
("p", "The verse speaks of belonging to the risen Christ, and this volume does not deny it. The question is what the illustration establishes. Its point is the release from the law and the fruit that follows. The woman of the illustration is bound by the law and set free by a death, and the application is that the believer is free from the law by the body of Christ. It does not mention a covenant oath, an espousal in the wilderness, a bill of divorce or Zion. It does not call the believers the wife of the Lamb, and it does not claim the prophets’ promises for them. The marriage in the verse is a figure for a change of master and for the fruit of the new life, and the illustration cannot be made to carry more than that."),
("sub", "10.2 2 Corinthians 11:2–3: the pastor’s jealousy"),
("p", "Paul writes to a church that is being drawn away by teachers he calls false, and he says, “For I am jealous over you with godly jealousy: for I have espoused you to one husband, that I may present you as a chaste virgin to Christ” (2 Corinthians 11:2). The picture is of a father or friend who has arranged a betrothal and who is anxious that the bride-to-be be found faithful. The fear is then stated: “as the serpent beguiled Eve through his subtilty, so your minds should be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ” (2 Corinthians 11:3)."),
("p", "Three things are plain. The figure is introduced with “as,” as a chaste virgin. The person who has done the espousing is Paul, and what he is jealous for is the Corinthians’ loyalty to Christ. And the comparison is made in a context of warning, so that its force is moral and pastoral. The figure of a virgin presented to her husband belongs to the family of figures that Isaiah uses (Isaiah 62:5), and the shared figure is no reason to merge the people. Paul does not say that the Corinthians are the wife of Isaiah 54 or the Lamb’s wife of Revelation 19. He says that he wants them to be as faithful to Christ as a chaste virgin is to her husband."),
("sub", "10.3 Ephesians 5:22–33: the household and the church"),
("p", "The longest of the three passages is addressed to wives and husbands. The wife is told to submit “as unto the Lord,” “for the husband is the head of the wife, even as Christ is the head of the church: and he is the saviour of the body” (Ephesians 5:22–23). The husbands are told, “love your wives, even as Christ also loved the church, and gave himself for it” (Ephesians 5:25), and the purpose of the giving is that he might “sanctify and cleanse it with the washing of water by the word,” and “present it to himself a glorious church, not having spot, or wrinkle, or any such thing; but that it should be holy and without blemish” (Ephesians 5:26–27)."),
("p", "This is the strongest passage for the reading that the church is the bride, and it deserves a careful reading. The pattern of the argument is a comparison of two loves. The husband’s love for his wife is set beside Christ’s love for the church. The word that joins them is “even as” (5:23, 25, 29). The words that Paul uses of the church are those of the body: “the saviour of the body,” “his own flesh,” “members of his body, of his flesh, and of his bones” (Ephesians 5:23, 29–30). The husband is to love his wife “as their own bodies” (Ephesians 5:28), because Christ loves the church, which is his body. The church is called a church, a body and an “it.” It is not once called the bride or the wife."),
("p", "Then Paul quotes the law of marriage, “For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall be joined unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh” (Ephesians 5:31; compare Genesis 2:24), and adds, “This is a great mystery: but I speak concerning Christ and the church” (Ephesians 5:32). The mystery is the union. In this letter the union of Christ and the church is a union of head and body (Ephesians 1:22–23; 4:15–16), and one flesh is the figure that shows how close it is. The verse that follows turns back to the household: “let every one of you in particular so love his wife even as himself” (Ephesians 5:33)."),
("tbl", "The table follows the three passages clause by clause.", [
    ("Rom 7:4", "“ye also are become dead to the law by the body of Christ; that ye should be married to another”", "The point is release from the law and fruit"),
    ("2 Cor 11:2", "“I have espoused you to one husband, that I may present you as a chaste virgin to Christ”", "The comparison is introduced with as"),
    ("2 Cor 11:3", "“as the serpent beguiled Eve through his subtilty”", "The context is a warning about loyalty"),
    ("Eph 5:23", "“the husband is the head of the wife, even as Christ is the head of the church”", "The comparison is of headship"),
    ("Eph 5:25", "“Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the church”", "The comparison is of love"),
    ("Eph 5:27", "“that he might present it to himself a glorious church”", "What is presented is called a church"),
    ("Eph 5:30", "“we are members of his body, of his flesh, and of his bones”", "The figure of the body carries the union"),
    ("Eph 5:32", "“This is a great mystery: but I speak concerning Christ and the church”", "The mystery is the union of Christ and the church"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "10.4 What the three uses do"),
("ctbl", "The three uses side by side.", [
    ("Passage", "What the figure is used for", "What it never says"),
    ("Romans 7:1–4", "To show release from the law by death and fruit to God", "That the believers are the wife of the prophets or of the Lamb"),
    ("2 Corinthians 11:2–3", "To show a pastor’s jealousy for loyalty to Christ", "That the Corinthians are the bride of Revelation 19 or 21"),
    ("Ephesians 5:22–33", "To teach husbands and wives and to show Christ’s love for the church", "That the church is called the bride or the wife"),
  ], [0.24, 0.40, 0.36]),
("sub", "10.5 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say that the Body is not joined to Christ or loved by him. The union is real, and Paul’s figures are the figures of the closest union he can name.",
    "It does not say that the three passages are only figures of speech and carry no weight. They carry the weight of their own instruction to husbands and wives, to pastors and churches, and to those who are released from the law.",
    "It does not say that Paul was ignorant of the prophets’ wife. It says that he does not give her name or her covenant to the Body.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "Paul’s three uses of marriage teach release from the law, loyalty to Christ and the love of husbands and wives. Where he speaks of the church he uses the words church, body and flesh, and where he uses a comparison he introduces it with as. He does not call the church the bride."),
]
